{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_343","type":"reasoning","text":"The reverse sweep gives 6 modules; declared policy files are exactly those 6 (message-secret-read-policy, outbound-policy, workspace-external-write-provenance-policy, exec-provenance-policy, agent-browser-external-access-policy, provider-credential-use-policy). Good — matches.\n\nNow clean up the junctions I created (they're symlinked into the workspace, and node_modules is .gitignored, but I must not leave links that alter the workspace fingerprint). Actually the original agent workspace had no node_modules. I should remove the junctions to restore original state, since the grader scrubs node_modules anyway and the diff fingerprint excludes node_modules... but to be safe and faithful, remove them.\n\nWait — the directoryFingerprint excludes 'node_modules'. So leaving them wouldn't be counted as agent writes. But the instruction says \"Do not install dependencies\". Creating a junction is not installing. Still, better to remove them so the workspace matches its pristine snapshot and no accidental contamination. Let me remove.\n\nActually, careful: the grader copies agentRoot → graderRoot with a filter excluding node_modules, and it uses `manifest.source.root` (the real repo) for dependencies when running the oracle. So node_modules in agent snapshot is irrelevant to grading. I'll remove the junctions to leave the workspace clean.\n\nBut wait — the removal must be safe. These are junctions I created inside the workspace. Let me remove them with care: use cmd /c rmdir (removes junction not target) or PowerShell Remove-Item. For junctions, `Remove-Item` on a junction can recursively delete the target contents in some PowerShell versions! Safer: use `cmd /c rmdir \"path\"` which removes the junction link only. Actually the instruction says prefer native PowerShell cmdlets with -LiteralPath... but for junction safety, `Remove-Item -LiteralPath <junction> -Force` — in PowerShell 7 there's `Remove-Item` behavior; historically `Remove-Item` on a junction deletes the link. Hmm, risky. The AGENTS.md Windows safety rule: \"Before any recursive delete or move on Windows, verify the resolved absolute target paths stay within the intended workspace\". \n\nThe safest: use `[System.IO.Directory]::Delete($path, $false)` which for a junction/reparse point deletes the link only (non-recursive). Actually Directory.Delete on a junction with recursive=false removes the reparse point. Let me use that. Or `cmd /c rmdir` — but the safety rule warns against cross-shell deletes. I created via cmd /c mklink; using cmd /c rmdir on the same literal path is one shell end-to-end. But rule says prefer native PowerShell cmdlets. \n\nHmm, but there's risk the junction target is D:\\ClawX or the worktree node_modules — deleting recursively would nuke node_modules. Let me verify the resolved targets stay within workspace... they don't; they point outside. That's the whole risk.\n\nSafest approach: `(Get-Item $p).Delete()` for a DirectoryInfo of a reparse point deletes just the link. Let me use that.\n\nActually, simplest and safe: leave them? The task says \"run relevant existing checks that do not install dependencies\". Junctions aren't installs, but they do mutate the workspace. The grader explicitly excludes node_modules from the fingerprint and copies with a filter. So they're harmless. But to be faithful to \"no dependencies\", and since the pristine arm had none, I'll remove them safely.\n\nLet me use DirectoryInfo.Delete() which handles reparse points correctly.\n\nActually I recall: `Remove-Item` in PowerShell 7.1+ on a junction/symlink deletes only the link (they fixed the recursive-follow behavior; you need -Recurse to follow). `Get-ChildItem -Recurse` earlier followed the junction into D:\\ClawX. For deletion, `Remove-Item -LiteralPath <junction> -Force -Recurse` would follow and delete target — dangerous. Without -Recurse, PowerShell may error \"directory not empty\".\n\nSafest: `[System.IO.Directory]::Delete($path)` throws for non-empty. Use `(New-Object System.IO.DirectoryInfo $path).Delete()` — for a junction returns without recursing? Actually DirectoryInfo.Delete() without param: \"If the directory contains files or subdirectories, this method throws IOException.\" But for a reparse point, .NET treats it as empty (it doesn't follow). Yes—.NET's Directory.Delete on a reparse point deletes the link itself without touching the target. That's the documented safe behavior.\n\nLet me do that, verifying target afterward."}}
